# Purpose
This document contains only project-specific frontend rules.
Generic rules: [Tech Doc](./tech-doc.mdc).
Product behavior: [Business Doc](./business-doc.mdc).
Related backend details: [Specific Tech Backend Doc](./specific-tech-backend-doc.mdc).

# Actual stack
- Blade server-rendered (no SPA framework).
- TypeScript in `resources/ts`.
- PostCSS in `resources/css`.
- Bundling/build with Vite.
- Frontend work is mobile-first: every new or changed workflow must remain usable on narrow screens, with responsive controls that do not overflow or become ambiguous.

## Google Analytics 4

Google tags are loaded from the shared Blade layout
(`resources/views/layouts/app.blade.php`) only when the Laravel environment is
`production` and the corresponding analytics configuration is set. Google Tag
Manager uses `config('analytics.gtm.container_id')`, stored in `config/analytics.php`
and read from the `GTM_CONTAINER_ID` environment variable. Its script is placed in
the document head and its `noscript` iframe immediately after the opening body tag.
GA4 uses `config('analytics.ga4.measurement_id')`, read from
`GA4_MEASUREMENT_ID`. These variables must be set in the production `.env`; they
should remain empty in local and test environments so development traffic is not
recorded.

## Multilingual SEO

The shared public layout (`resources/views/layouts/app.blade.php`) renders
`hreflang` links for the equivalent localized version of the current public
route. The route equivalence is resolved centrally by
`App\Support\RouteHelper::alternates()` from `config/routes.php`; templates
must not build hreflang URLs manually. The Italian equivalent is emitted as
`x-default`.

Thank-you pages and token-based pages such as online check-in and guest review
do not expose hreflang links because they are transactional or not public
localized content. When changing supported languages or public URL slugs,
update `config/routes.php`, translations and route tests, then verify both the
hreflang output and `sitemap.xml`.

## Homepage content sections

`resources/views/public/home.blade.php` renders, in order: hero (H1 in
`app.hero_title` uses keyword-targeted copy per locale, e.g. "Ferienwohnung in
Andora, Ligurien" for DE, "Italian Riviera" for EN — rendered with `{!! !!}`
because it contains a `<br>` after the comma for a cleaner desktop line
break), a conditional trust-badges section, the booking section, the apartment
feature-highlights section, and the localized SEO text block.

The trust-badges section (`config('apartment.trust_badges')`, same
`['icon' => ..., 'key' => ...]` shape as `apartment.features`) only renders
when the internal direct-booking form is active — it is wrapped in
`@unless ($bookingMode === 'external' && $bookingExternalUrl)`, mirroring the
exact condition used to pick the booking UI further down the page. Its
"transparent costs" badge intentionally does not claim "no fees" since private
parking is paid. The feature-highlights section (heading `app.home_features_title`)
always renders regardless of booking mode. Both reuse the same `.home-features`
CSS grid in `resources/css/pages/home.css` (2 columns from the smallest
viewport, 4 columns from 1024px) — do not add a third near-identical grid
without a heading, as two headless/identical grids stacked back-to-back read
as one undifferentiated list.

This is part of a multi-phase SEO/copy initiative tracked in
`openspec/changes/seo-international-optimization/` (see its `tasks.md` for
remaining phases: pain-point sections, guide pages, sitemap/schema
enrichment).

## Vacation rental structured data

The localized public home emits one `application/ld+json` block from
`resources/views/components/schema-vacation-rental.blade.php`, included through
the `schema` stack in `resources/views/public/home.blade.php`. It describes the
property as a Schema.org `VacationRental` with an `Accommodation`, address,
coordinates, images, occupancy, bedrooms, bathrooms, rooms, floor size, exact
bed types, amenities, and configured check-in/check-out times.

The component must remain data-driven. Stable property facts come from
`config/apartment.php` (`schema`, `address`, `specs`, `amenities`, `images`, and
`booking`); localized page descriptions come from the locale-specific SEO
configuration. The stable identifier in `apartment.schema.identifier` must be
the same for every locale. Do not add generic prices or availability to this
static markup: both depend on requested dates and guest counts. When changing
any property fact, image, amenity, bed detail, room count, surface, address,
coordinate, or check-in/check-out time, update this schema's source data and
its feature test together.

## Admin Telegram recipients

The admin user edit view (`resources/views/admin/users/edit.blade.php`) exposes the
`telegram_notifications_enabled` checkbox alongside the Telegram chat ID. A recipient
must have both values configured. When the role changes, the backend resets the checkbox
according to the new role default; the helper text in the form explains this behavior.

## Admin booking notification recipients

The booking detail workflow (`resources/views/admin/bookings/show.blade.php`) displays
the effective recipients below each send action and includes them in the confirmation
dialog. Email recipients are prepared by `BookingController::show`: the guest is the
`To` recipient, `config('apartment.email')` is the BCC, and booking confirmation also
lists the `host_keeper` users who receive the separate operational email. Telegram
recipients are users with both a non-null `telegram_chat_id` and
`telegram_notifications_enabled = true`; the UI shows their names, or an explicit
empty-recipient message.

The numbered steps in the booking workflow turn green independently when the
corresponding action is genuinely complete: confirmation email sent,
payment-received email sent, online check-in completed, or a definitive
Alloggiati Web submission recorded with `mode = send` and `status = success`.
Successful draft tests do not mark the reporting step complete.

## Booking reviews

Review management is accessed from the booking detail page as step 5 of the
booking workflow, and is visible only to users with `manage_reviews`. The
action is contextual: it offers `Aggiungi recensione` when no review exists,
otherwise `Modifica` and `Elimina`. The global admin sidebar does not contain a
separate reviews entry.

The review form accepts a rating from 1 to 10 and stores optional translated
feedback for what guests liked and did not like. The rating is displayed as a
numeric `X/10` score in the public page and admin summaries. The two feedback
blocks appear only when their localized text is available, using a green smile
for liked feedback and a dark sad face for disliked feedback.

